00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
the stock market hit major turbulence  this week nursing some steep losses and  sharp rebounds following a rocky start  to the new year it is a far cry from the  study gains that we saw through most of  2021. so when the federal reserve's  policymakers met today investors were  counting on the central bank to explain  how it's going to address rising  inflation to get insight into what came  out of the meeting and how that will  affect the stock market let's bring in  bloomberg's bailey lip shultz bailey  thanks so much for joining us i want to  start on the anticipation heading into  today's fed meeting did central banker  jay powell provide enough clarity for  investors moving forward  yes and no they came out saying that  investors should expect a rate hike soon  you should read that as march but the  main question from investors was how  rapid will these rate hikes uh take  place and from fedshare powell they  basically left the door open for  multiple rate hikes at every meeting or  excuse me a rate hike at every meeting  and that caused some concern for  investors because they had gotten so  accustomed to jay powell on the fed  using some uh typical language that  assured the market of a gradual approach  everything was going to take place at a  very calculated pace and some of the  commentary in the question and answer  today with fed chair powell was that  investors really should buckle up for  the potential of a rate hike  consecutively at meeting after meeting  okay so he signaled that we could get  steady rate increases but there was also  a lot of uncertainty about what that  might look like going forward because we  don't know how inflation will shake out  explain to me why higher interest rates  would prompt stocks to fall though  well when you look at investing so  typically  interest rates when they're higher make  stock markets less attractive and that's  really what we've been seeing when you  look back to november when fed uh  initially kind of signaled that  investors should get ready for uh  additional interest rate hikes in 2022  and 2023 that's why we saw a lot of the  high-tech uh high-growth tech stocks  really fall under pressure those are  companies that are preferred uh by the  likes of kathy wood um read that as a  company like a robin hood or  non-profitable tech companies because  when you're investing when interest  rates are higher those profits that are  coming in 2024 2025 and 2026 look less  attractive in the near term and that  really has been the main messaging from  investors that i've been talking to and  inflation is only going up the latest  read shows consumer prices rising seven  percent it seems like a lot of people  have been caught by surprise at how  persistent it is when prices first  started rising there was this fun debate  over whether it's temporary transitory  now it seems pretty clear that it's here  to stay but what is the investor  confidence that raising interest rates  is going to cool inflation moving  forward  it's really mixed because there are  still concerns around supply chain  supply chains were initially a big  factor that drove inflation and drove  products that you and individual  consumers were buying higher and now  we're seeing not only producers or not  only  sellers raising prices but they're kind  of passing along those costs to in  consumers and that's one of the things  that investors are unsure of when you  look at it from a generalist investing  standpoint uh rate hikes should help  ease some of the inflation but we're  still kind of in uncharted territory  just given the unprecedented nature of a  pandemic like we saw with covet 19 as we  continue to come out of it and as  employment really runs near  what said chair jay powell said is  likely maximum employment yeah the  reasons behind the inflation are  different this time because we're in a  pandemic that and we haven't seen this  kind of situation play out before in a  long time  billy there are obviously a lot of cross  currents when it comes to the stock  market interest rates and what the  federal reserve says and does is just  one part of the picture  you've also got companies reporting  financial earnings and you mentioned a  couple of them earlier we're getting a  lot of earnings coming in this week has  been super busy for all the companies  that are reporting uh their financial  results it's kind of like giving the  report card to investors can you distill  an overarching message from the  companies that have reported banks like  jp morgan or  tech companies like microsoft what is  the message from them  the big takeaway from banks is that  they're spending a lot of money to  retain talent and they're keeping a  close eye on what consumers are doing  whether that's taking on debt to go out  and buy a new car or maybe make a huge  purchase like buying a house  that really was the main message coming  from banks but when you look at a  company like microsoft yesterday really  shot the lights out with stellar  earnings growth a seller outlook for  2022 it came at the right time for a lot  of these tech companies that are  invested uh by pretty much everyone in  the in the us whether you're looking at  a microsoft or a facebook a google those  companies are continuing to deliver and  it'll be particularly interesting this  week as we get results from an apple  looking at what the outlook looks like  as these companies kind of go through  any supply chain issues and make real  broad statements on where they see  consumers in 2022 but obviously the  market is just about 10 off record highs  hit earlier in the year so there is  still a little bit of trepidation about  what growth will look like and how  active u.s consumers will be in spending  well also the stock market as a whole  has had a lot of blockbuster years from  2019 to 2020-2021  what are the experts you talked to  saying about the likelihood of stocks  finding their footing to post yet  another year of gains  the main thing that i'm hearing from  people that i'm talking to on wall  street is that there should still be  optimism that 2022 can deliver gains and  end the year higher than where it came  in the main question is the near term  we're hearing from a lot of portfolio  managers saying they expect a choppy  next two three four months i was talking  to a couple people who helped uh take  companies public and they said they're  really setting their sights on what  april will bring what will the economy  look like in april here in the u.s and  globally as we potentially deal with uh  future variants of covid as people  continue to adjust to a potentially or  another likely rate hike from an  investment standpoint and as we really  get a better grip on what the u.s  economy and what the global economy is  looking like again as we continue to  hopefully step forward um from what has  been a brutal pandemic all things being  cleared  you know one thing that we've seen since  uh the bottom of the stock market is  that every time you see some kind of dip  we see buyers come in an army of buyers  come in to bid prices higher it's called  buying the dip right and it's worked so  well over the last couple of years this  year however we're looking at the stock  market nursing losses for 2022. why is  buy the dip not working  we're not seeing is the same rush from  both retail investors and wall street  pros there's a lot of uncertainty about  again what will the next month two  months three months look like we are  hearing from retail traders that they  are putting money into broad etfs that  track things like the s p 500 things  that track the nasdaq 100 but we're not  seeing the robust demand to buy a dip  and snatch up a dip because again there  still is so much uncertainty of what the  fed will do how will the real economy be  shaped by fed rate [ __ ] uh by inflation  there's just a general uh uncertainty  and obviously the market doesn't like  uncertainty but scarlett one thing i  personally am uh really keen to see is  what will we see on monday and tuesday  of next week as it is the end of the  month in the beginning of february when  people tend to get paid we normally see  retail investors buy more dips when  checks hit their bank accounts so  that'll be an interesting signal for me  for me to see if some of those retail  investors will be stepping back in like  we have seen for really the last year  bailey do you anticipate individual  investors giving up on stocks and moving  into other investments like commodities  nfts crypto  uh it doesn't seem to be that way i talk  to a lot of individual investors who are  on things like twitter stock twits  wall street bets and if you are into the  cryptocurrency space or if you're into  something like nfts there is definitely  a demand from people who are into that  kind of an asset but generally speaking  what i'm seeing and hearing more from  individual investors are they're selling  ideas of the kathy woods they're selling  these high-flying growth stocks that  they have seen shoot up over the last  few years and are really putting their  money into kind of easy calm investments  like again ets that track the broader  market etfs that have a little bit of  balance even if they're tech skewed  because it's easier to kind of put cash  in make sure your money's going into  that and not worry too much about the  ups and downs especially if you're an  individual investor with a longer term  horizon who doesn't necessarily sweat  the uh 10 correction in a month all  right bailey well thank you so much for  joining us and giving us the much needed  context on that meeting today really  appreciate it that was bailey lip  schultz joining us for more on the feds  meeting today  you

